# FoxtrotEngine C++ Conventions

FoxtrotEngine is a Windows-only, DirectX 11, component-based 2D game engine (C++17, Visual Studio 2022). Match existing patterns in `Engine/` before introducing new ones.

## Project Layout

- `Engine/Common` — shared interfaces, FTDS data structures, base types
- `Engine/Core` — actors, scenes, managers, chunk I/O, game loop
- `Engine/D3D11` — DirectX 11 renderer and graphics components
- `Engine/Editor` — ImGui editor (Command pattern for undo/redo)
- `Engine/Graphics` — renderer/resource abstractions (interfaces)
- `Engine/Math`, `Engine/InputSystem`, `Engine/GenericData` — supporting libs

### Each projects, except for Editor will build .dll

## Naming & Formatting

- **Classes:** PascalCase (`Actor`, `ResourceManager`)
- **Private members:** `mCamelCase` (`mOwner`, `mComponents`)
- **Methods:** PascalCase (`GetComponent`, `AddChild`)
- **Constants:** `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE`; **namespaces:** PascalCase
- **Headers:** `#pragma once`, GPL v3 copyright block, `/// <summary>` docs
- **Style:** Allman braces, tabs (4-wide), left pointer alignment (`int* ptr`) — follow `.clang-format`

## Memory & Ownership

- Use `DBG_NEW` for allocations; raw `new`/`delete`, no smart pointers
- Clean up in destructors; singletons must call `Destroy()` on shutdown

```cpp
// ✅ GOOD
mInstance = DBG_NEW ResourceManager();

// ❌ BAD
auto mgr = std::make_unique<ResourceManager>();
```

## Architecture Patterns

**Singleton managers** — use `SINGLETON` / `SINGLETON_PROTECTED` macros:

```cpp
SINGLETON(ResourceManager)
ResourceManager::GetInstance()->LoadTexture(...);
```

**Actor-Component** — actors own components; prefer interfaces in `Common`:

```cpp
actor->AddComponent<SpriteRenderer>();
actor->GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>()->SetVelocity(...);
```

**Component lifecycle:** `Initialize` → `Setup` → `ProcessInput` → `Update` → `LateUpdate` → `Render`

**Editor commands** — inherit from `Command`, execute via `CommandHistory::GetInstance()`

## Serialization & Data

- Scene/resource data uses `.chunk` files via `ChunkLoader`
- Serialization keys live in `Common::ChunkKey` namespaces
- Use `Common::FTDS::String`, `DynamicArray`, etc. — not STL containers where FTDS equivalents exist

## When Adding Code

- Place files in the correct module; keep `.h`/`.cpp` paired in the same directory
- Extend existing interfaces (`IActor`, `IComponent`, `IRenderer`) rather than duplicating logic
- Prefer minimal, focused diffs; do not refactor unrelated code
- Windows/DX11 only — no cross-platform abstractions unless explicitly requested